    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 162103\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n203 PM PDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Isolated-to-scattered showers and thunderstorms arrive this \n  afternoon and linger into Thursday morning with a focus across our \n  eastern zones.\n\n* Elsewhere, expect breezy to south-southwesterly wind gusts until \n  the weekend with dry conditions and near-normal temperatures.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Tuesday.\n\nShower and storm activity looks to build this afternoon in Mohave Co \nwith initial convection starting southeast of Lake Havasu and \nspreading north through the late afternoon, and reaching Lincoln Co \ninto the night. Fairly strong CAPE is making a good environment for \nthunderstorm growth, but the lack of sheer may prevent some storms \nfrom reaching severe status. The main threat from these storms looks \nto be the potential for flooding as storms could train over certain \nareas. Additional threats will be frequent lighting and locally \nstrong gusts from stronger cells. While Mohave and Lincoln counties \nlook to be the focus of the convection, isolated cells could cross \ninto Clark Co, along with a few storms building along higher terrain \nin western Clark Co into Nye Co. Shower activity will stay with \nLincoln and Clark counties through the night and through much of \nThursday morning before dissipating.\n\n\nThe active weather this afternoon and through the night comes along \na pattern shift with a low passing over the area and a period of \ntroughing to follow. This pattern will favor windy conditions today \nand tonight with widespread winds reaching 20 mph and gusts peaking \naround 35 mph. Some of the favored gaps and passes along with higher \nterrain could briefly exceed 40 mph gusts. Winds look to reduce \nslightly for most areas into the night, but the higher terrain will \nstay windy. Widespread gusts will pickup again Thursday afternoon, \nbut uniformly reduce that night. Friday afternoon looks to be breezy \nas well, but overall winds ease into the weekend itself. The \ntroughing pattern will keep high temperatures close to average for \nthis time of year, but lows will still trend on the warmer side.\n\n\n\n\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...\nGusty SE winds have begun across the Las Vegas Valley today. Similar \nto yesterday, a stronger easterly pressure gradient over the area \nmay keep the easterly wind component around through mid afternoon. \nConfidence is low on the exact hour of the wind shift to S-SW, but \ncurrent thinking is that it should occur after 20z, potentially as \nlate as 22z. Expect gusts 20-25 kt while winds are SE, then gusts up \nto 30 kt possible late evening through 03z after the SSW shift \noccurs. Late afternoon and evening thunderstorm activity is expected \nto remain east of the terminal over northwestern Arizona and the \nMormon Mesa corridor, with VFR conditions prevailing. Not currently \nexpecting outflow from storms to impact the Valley, but will monitor \nstorm and outflow evolution closely. FEW-SCT clouds with bases \naround 10kft can be expected this afternoon and tonight, with \ntemperatures remaining under 100 degrees.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Southeast winds are \nincreasing late this morning with gusts exceeding 20 kt in the CRV. \nExpect a shift to the S-SW around 20-22z. Gusts to around 25KT will \nbe common across the region, with stronger gusts to around 30KT in \nthe northern Owens Valley and along the Lower Colorado River Valley \nwhere up-valley winds develop. KDAG is the usual exception with W \nwinds around 10 kt persisting through the day, then expect the WSW \npush around and after 23z with gusts 20-25+ kt into the early \novernight hours. Elsewhere, speeds and gusts diminish after sunset, \nbut hi-res guidance continues to indicate elevated winds and \nisolated gusts for the CRV overnight with the potential for impacts \nfrom outflow boundaries. Thunderstorms are expected this afternoon \nand evening over eastern areas, and especially over northwestern \nArizona, with impacts from lightning and gusty outflow winds \npossible at both KEED and KIFP. FEW-SCT clouds with bases at or \nabove 10kft are expected this afternoon onward for most of the \nregion, with lower ceilings and greater coverage in the vicinity of \nthunderstorms.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...\nAVIATION...Moore\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
